The Water Court of the Great Oasis was the earliest stable hydraulic legal authority of the eastern corridor.
It governed volumetric rights through institutions including the Cistern Ring and Charter of Passage formulas.
Its core innovation was measurement-as-law: water volume, timing, and witnessed allocation became enforceable civic order.
Later corridor powers inherited Water Court legal language even when their theology and politics diverged.
